think THE CENSUS HAD ALREADY NAMED THE TAIL, so this lap needed no search. section_citation stood at 1,010 execve and 539 per mille in the fan-out sample; traced alone it reads 1,015 processes, 962 of them grep, which is 95 percent in one tool.
think The site is ONE line. Line 98 ran grep -qxF once per tracked Markdown page, asking whether a basename stood in a list the same script had written moments earlier. That is the exact idiom cured twice already today in standing_equipment_scan.sh, where three such sites were 916 of 974 processes.
think Checked at the door rather than assumed: derived.txt is written before the loop opens and never appended to inside it, so one read serves every iteration; the loop sets no positional parameters; and the file holds one basename per line with no surrounding whitespace. The newlines wrapping both the set and the needle are what keep the match whole-line exactly as grep -x was.
obs EVERY PREDICTION HELD. Byte-identical was the wall and it held, exit 0 unchanged. I predicted under 100 processes and it reads 54. I predicted the wall would at least halve and it fell 4,388ms to 1,027ms, which is 4.3x. The rostered witness is GREEN.
obs THE COMPARISON WAS MADE AGAINST A HELD-STILL TREE from the start, because this lap met that false alarm earlier: a baseline captured before another guard ran differed only because the guard had rewritten a file the scan reads. Stash, run, unstash, run, compare -- both versions in the same moment against the same tree.
obs THE IDIOM IS NOW THREE FOR THREE and the shape is worth naming as a class rather than three repairs: a membership test or a field read, inside a loop, answered by spawning a tool for something the shell already does. Across three files today it accounted for 4,137 of 5,345 processes.
obs root_finder IS MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. Its 904 processes are 515 tr and 265 awk across two different shapes -- one value recomputed three times per loop iteration, and a map lookup spawning awk per call -- so it is its own lap rather than a tail on this one.
